---
import CostLayout from '../../layouts/CostLayout.astro';
import RegionalCosts from '../../components/RegionalCosts.astro';
import RelatedCosts from '../../components/RelatedCosts.astro';
import { COST_SOURCES, MAINTENANCE, range } from '../../data/costs';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'How much does pool maintenance cost per month?',
    a: 'Doing it yourself runs $80 to $150 a month once chemicals, electricity and water are counted. A weekly professional service runs $150 to $300. The national average across both sits near $140 a month.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is a pool service worth it?',
    a: 'It costs roughly $1,000 to $2,400 a year more than doing it yourself. Whether that is worth it depends on your time and how much you enjoy the work — but a service that catches a chemistry problem early can also prevent a resurfacing bill years ahead of schedule.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the biggest monthly pool cost?',
    a: 'Electricity for the pump, usually $50 to $100 a month, and often more than chemicals. A variable-speed pump is the single largest saving available, because power draw falls roughly with the cube of speed.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How much do pool chemicals cost per month?',
    a: 'Typically $30 to $60 a month for a residential pool, more in peak summer and less in the off-season. Overdosing because of an inaccurate pool volume is a common and entirely avoidable way to inflate that figure.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does a saltwater pool cost less to maintain?',
    a: 'Month to month, yes — often around $45 in DIY chemical costs against $50 to $80 for a chlorine pool. The offset is the salt cell, which costs several hundred dollars and needs replacing every three to seven years.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How can I lower my pool maintenance costs?',
    a: 'Run a variable-speed pump at low speed for longer, use a cover to cut evaporation and chemical loss, keep the filter clean so the pump works less, and dose against an accurate pool volume so nothing is wasted.',
  },
];
---

<CostLayout
  title="Monthly Pool Maintenance Cost in 2026: DIY vs Service"
  description="Pool maintenance costs $80–$150 monthly for DIY and $150–$300 for a weekly service. See the full breakdown and where the money actually goes."
  h1="Monthly Pool Maintenance Cost"
  answer="Maintaining a pool yourself costs $80 to $150 a month once chemicals, electricity and water are counted. A weekly professional service runs $150 to $300 a month. The national average across both sits near $140. Electricity for the pump is usually the largest single item."
  path="/cost/monthly-maintenance/"
  breadcrumbName="Monthly Maintenance Cost"
  publishDate={new Date('2026-08-03')}
  faqs={faqs}
  sources={[COST_SOURCES.homeguideMaintenance, COST_SOURCES.homeAdvisorMaintenance]}
>
  <div slot="table">
    <h2 class="text-2xl">Monthly cost, DIY versus a service</h2>

    <div class="mt-6 overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full border-collapse text-left">
        <thead>
          <tr class="border-b-2 border-navy-200">
            <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-display text-sm uppercase tracking-wider text-navy-500">
              Approach
            </th>
            <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-display text-sm uppercase tracking-wider text-navy-500">
              Per month
            </th>
            <th class="py-3 font-display text-sm uppercase tracking-wider text-navy-500">
              Per year
            </th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr class="border-b border-navy-100">
            <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-navy-900">Doing it yourself</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-display font-semibold text-blue-600">
              {range(MAINTENANCE.diy.low, MAINTENANCE.diy.high)}
            </td>
            <td class="py-3 text-navy-500">
              {range(MAINTENANCE.diy.low * 12, MAINTENANCE.diy.high * 12)}
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-navy-100">
            <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-navy-900">Weekly professional service</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-display font-semibold text-blue-600">
              {range(MAINTENANCE.professional.low, MAINTENANCE.professional.high)}
            </td>
            <td class="py-3 text-navy-500">
              {range(MAINTENANCE.professional.low * 12, MAINTENANCE.professional.high * 12)}
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-navy-500">
      A service typically covers weekly visits and routine chemicals. It usually
      excludes algae recovery, equipment repair, and seasonal opening and
      closing — worth confirming before signing.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div>
    <h2 class="text-2xl">Where the money actually goes</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-navy-600">
      Most people assume chemicals dominate. For most pools, electricity does.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-6 overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full border-collapse text-left">
        <thead>
          <tr class="border-b-2 border-navy-200">
            <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-display text-sm uppercase tracking-wider text-navy-500">
              Item
            </th>
            <th class="py-3 font-display text-sm uppercase tracking-wider text-navy-500">
              Per month
            </th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            MAINTENANCE.breakdown.map((row) => (
              <tr class="border-b border-navy-100">
                <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-navy-900">{row.item}</td>
                <td class="py-3 font-display font-semibold text-blue-600">
                  {range(row.low, row.high)}
                </td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
          <tr class="border-b-2 border-navy-200">
            <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-display font-bold text-navy-900">Total</td>
            <td class="py-3 font-display font-bold text-navy-900">
              {range(
                MAINTENANCE.breakdown.reduce((sum, row) => sum + row.low, 0),
                MAINTENANCE.breakdown.reduce((sum, row) => sum + row.high, 0),
              )}
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-navy-600">
      The pump is the largest line, which makes it the biggest lever. A
      variable-speed pump running longer at low speed moves the same water for a
      fraction of the power, because consumption scales with roughly the cube of
      speed.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div>
    <h2 class="text-2xl">What is not in the monthly figure</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-navy-600">
      Routine cost is the easy part to budget. These are the items that arrive
      irregularly and catch people out.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-6 overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full border-collapse text-left">
        <thead>
          <tr class="border-b-2 border-navy-200">
            <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-display text-sm uppercase tracking-wider text-navy-500">
              Item
            </th>
            <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-display text-sm uppercase tracking-wider text-navy-500">
              Cost
            </th>
            <th class="py-3 font-display text-sm uppercase tracking-wider text-navy-500">
              How often
            </th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            [
              ['Opening and closing', '$300–$700 each', 'Twice a year in cold climates'],
              ['Filter media replacement', '$100–$800', 'Sand every 5–7 yrs, cartridges every 2–5'],
              ['Salt cell replacement', '$400–$1,200', 'Every 3–7 years'],
              ['Pump replacement', '$800–$2,500', 'Every 8–12 years'],
              ['Heater replacement', '$2,000–$6,000', 'Every 8–15 years'],
              ['Resurfacing', '$6,000–$15,000', 'Every 7–25 years by finish'],
            ].map(([item, cost, frequency]) => (
              <tr class="border-b border-navy-100">
                <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-navy-900">{item}</td>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-display font-semibold text-blue-600">{cost}</td>
                <td class="py-3 text-sm text-navy-500">{frequency}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-navy-600">
      Averaged out, these add roughly $80 to $200 a month to the true cost of
      owning a pool. Setting that aside monthly is far more comfortable than
      meeting a resurfacing bill from nowhere.
    </p>
  </div>

  <RegionalCosts />

  <div>
    <h2 class="text-2xl">How to reduce the cost</h2>
    <ul class="mt-5 space-y-3">
      {
        [
          'Fit a variable-speed pump. It is the largest single saving available on a pool, and often pays for itself within two or three seasons.',
          'Use a cover. It cuts evaporation, which cuts both water and the chemicals that leave with it, and it slashes heating cost.',
          'Keep the filter clean. A dirty filter makes the pump work harder for less flow, which shows up directly on the electricity bill.',
          'Dose against an accurate pool volume. Guessing high wastes chemicals every single week; guessing low means fixing problems that should never have started.',
          'Buy chemicals in bulk before summer. Prices climb in peak season.',
        ].map((item) => (
          <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
            <svg
              class="mt-0.5 h-5 w-5 shrink-0 text-blue-500"
              fill="none"
              stroke="currentColor"
              stroke-width="2.5"
              viewBox="0 0 24 24"
            >
              <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" />
            </svg>
            <span class="text-navy-600">{item}</span>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
  </div>

  <RelatedCosts slot="related" exclude="monthly-maintenance" />
</CostLayout>
